# One tip per line; a new terminal shows one at random.
The Super key opens the launcher; type to find a program.
Right click on the desktop opens a menu with the main tools.
The camera icon in the panel shows your snapshots.
Super+Enter opens a terminal and Super+Q closes the focused window.
Print saves a screenshot to ~/Pictures.
snap now -c "before the update" names a snapshot so you find it later.
snap diff ID shows what changed since a snapshot.
snap restore ID PATH brings back a single file from a snapshot.
snap pin ID keeps a snapshot from ever being pruned.
wh install --try PKG is a trial: run wh keep, or it goes back later.
wh undo reverts the last package change.
wh install --from kali PKG takes a package from Kali's archive.
Programs from wormholes run on the desktop like any other app.
bh open gives you a sandbox with no network and no access to your files.
bh put NAME FILE copies a file into a blackhole's inbox.
worm panic cuts the network, closes all blackholes and takes a snapshot.
worm anon on sends all traffic through Tor.
worm fw allow 22 opens a port; everything else coming in is blocked.
The boot menu can rewind to any recent snapshot before anything starts.
The system is read-only: worm unlock opens it, worm lock snapshots it.
Ctrl+Alt+F2 has a second console, for logging in as root.
wlr-randr lists your screens and their modes.
Grey text after the cursor is an earlier command: Right or End takes it.
Up and Down go through earlier commands starting with what you typed.
Ctrl+R searches everything you typed before; Ctrl+Backspace deletes a word.
Tab after snap rewind, wh remove or bh close completes the name or number.
ls | clip copy puts a command's output on the clipboard; clip paste prints it.
Start a command with a space to keep it out of the history.
The prompt's [snap N] is your newest snapshot.
worm kit list shows tool kits (forensics, network, reverse engineering, web...); worm kit install NAME adds one (several at once, or all).
Open a suspicious file in a blackhole: bh put lab file.bin, then bh open lab. It has no network and cannot see your files.
worm panic cuts the network, stops every blackhole and keeps a snapshot for later analysis.
Programs in a blackhole get a terminal of their own: they cannot type into yours or set your clipboard.
